{
  "title": "Countercurrent concentration and ADH",
  "sources": [
    "CH37S45",
    "CH37S46",
    "CH37S52",
    "CH37S53",
    "CH37S55"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Opposite limbs",
      "text": "Follow the filtrate through the loop of Henle. Its two limbs run beside each other, but they do not handle water and solute in the same way."
    },
    {
      "start": 8.38,
      "title": "Build the gradient",
      "text": "The supplied chapter describes active sodium and chloride transport from the thick ascending limb into the medullary interstitium. This helps create a concentrated environment around the loop."
    },
    {
      "start": 21.22,
      "title": "Descending water loss",
      "text": "Water can then leave the water-permeable descending limb, concentrating the fluid that remains inside."
    },
    {
      "start": 27.32,
      "title": "Ascending dilution",
      "text": "In the ascending limb, solute leaves while water is retained, so the tubular fluid becomes more dilute."
    },
    {
      "start": 33.9,
      "title": "Final ADH adjustment",
      "text": "Finally, follow the fluid toward the collecting duct. Antidiuretic hormone increases water permeability there. More water returns to the body, and final urine volume decreases. Keep the two ideas separate: the loop builds the concentration gradient, while antidiuretic hormone regulates the final water adjustment. Motion, colors, and quantities are schematic."
    }
  ],
